This project explores foundational stochastic processes and numerical methods used in quantitative finance. 
I simulate Brownian motion using Gaussian increments and analyze its pathwise variation properties across increasingly fine time partitions, demonstrating the behavior of total, quadratic, and higher-order variation.

I then implement the Cox-Ross-Rubinstein (CRR) binomial model to price European put options and evaluate its convergence toward the Black-Scholes-Merton benchmark. 
The analysis examines pricing error as the number of time steps increases and uses log-log regression to estimate the rate at which binomial approximation uncertainty declines.
